SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Executive Order 14074 - Advancing Effective, Accountable Policing and Criminal Justice Practices To Enhance Public Trust and Public Safety","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-05-25","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/executive-order-14074-advancing-effective-accountable-policing-and-criminal-justice","claim":"Biden made accountability for misconduct, transparency through data, and anti-profiling central to federal policing reform.","locator":"Section 1, lines 79-80","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential text with clear dated policy language and direct attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 presidential policy action.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden action as President.","contrary_evidence":["The order mainly governs federal agencies and offers only indirect support for state and local policing reform."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.5","3B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Executive Order 14074 - Advancing Effective, Accountable Policing and Criminal Justice Practices To Enhance Public Trust and Public Safety","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-05-25","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/executive-order-14074-advancing-effective-accountable-policing-and-criminal-justice","claim":"The order also directed ending discriminatory pretextual stops and supporting responses for people experiencing domestic violence, sexual assault, trafficking, disability, homelessness, and other vulnerability.","locator":"Section 1, lines 81-82","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential text with specific protected-group and victim-safety language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 presidential policy action.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden action as President.","contrary_evidence":["Implementation depended on federal agencies and guidance rather than direct control of local police."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.7","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"FACT SHEET: Biden-Harris Administration Highlights Accomplishments on Anniversary of Historic Executive Order to Advance Effective, Accountable Policing and Strengthen Public Safety","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2023-05-25","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fact-sheet-biden-harris-administration-highlights-accomplishments-anniversary-historic","claim":"The administration said EO 14074 required bans on chokeholds, restrictions on no-knock warrants, body cameras, de-escalation training, misconduct reporting, and limits on military equipment transfers to local police.","locator":"Lines 63-64","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary administration summary of the EO's implemented federal policing requirements.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective implementation evidence for a 2022 policy.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden administration implementation of a Biden order.","contrary_evidence":["The fact sheet itself shows the strongest effects were on federal policing, with local adoption dependent on support and guidance."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.5","3B.6"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"National Law Enforcement Accountability Database","publisher":"Bureau of Justice Statistics","publication_date":"2024-12-19","url":"https://bjs.ojp.gov/national-law-enforcement-accountability-database","claim":"BJS explains that NLEAD was launched to strengthen hiring practices and provide a centralized repository of federal misconduct records, a direct outgrowth of EO 14074.","locator":"Overview, lines 138-140","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Government statistics page summarizing an implemented accountability mechanism tied to the 2022 EO.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective implementation evidence for a 2022 order.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden-order follow-through through DOJ/BJS.","contrary_evidence":["The database launched in late 2023, so it is implementation evidence rather than contemporaneous 2022 performance."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.5"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"FACT SHEET: National Law Enforcement Accountability Database","publisher":"U.S. Department of Justice","publication_date":"2024-12-19","url":"https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/fact-sheet-national-law-enforcement-accountability-database","claim":"The Justice Department says NLEAD is a centralized repository of misconduct and commendation records meant to improve transparency and accountability in federal law enforcement hiring and promotion.","locator":"Lines 138-140","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary DOJ implementation summary with explicit accountability function and launch date.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective implementation evidence for a 2022 directive.","ruler_attribution":"Implemented under Biden's 2022 EO 14074.","contrary_evidence":["It does not directly regulate state and local policing behavior."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.5"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Public Law 117-159 - Bipartisan Safer Communities Act","publisher":"GovInfo","publication_date":"2022-06-25","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/app/details/PLAW-117publ159","claim":"Congress and the President enacted the Bipartisan Safer Communities Act on June 25, 2022, an act explicitly titled to make communities safer.","locator":"Lines 75-81","source_type":"legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official public-law record with enactment date and title.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 enactment.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden signature on a federal domestic-safety law.","contrary_evidence":["The act was partial gun-safety reform, not a comprehensive solution to U.S. gun violence."],"lenses":["3B.6","3B.7","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bipartisan Safer Communities Act (P.L. 117-159): Section-by-Section Summary","publisher":"Congress.gov / CRS","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/R47310","claim":"CRS says the BSCA was enacted after Buffalo and Uvalde and that its funding supports mental health services, crisis response, school-based mental health, and suicide prevention.","locator":"Lines 576-580","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Congressional Research Service summary is authoritative for statutory scope and legislative context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 enactment and its immediate legislative rationale.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden signature plus congressional enactment context.","contrary_evidence":["CRS also shows the law was targeted and limited, not a broad domestic-safety reset."],"lenses":["3B.6","3B.7","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Remarks on Signing the Bipartisan Safer Communities Act and an Exchange With Reporters","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-06-25","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/remarks-signing-the-bipartisan-safer-communities-act-and-exchange-reporters","claim":"Biden publicly signed the gun-safety bill into law and said it would save lives.","locator":"Lines 82-88","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential signing remarks, dated and explicit about the policy intent.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 domestic-safety action.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden signature and statement.","contrary_evidence":["The bill addressed only part of the wider gun-violence problem."],"lenses":["3B.6","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement on the Shooting in Buffalo, New York","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-05-14","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/statement-the-shooting-buffalo-new-york","claim":"Biden called the Buffalo attack a racially motivated hate crime and said domestic terrorism in the name of white nationalist ideology is antithetical to American values.","locator":"Line 63","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential statement made immediately after the attack.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 crisis response.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden rhetoric and framing after a domestic mass shooting.","contrary_evidence":["The statement is rhetorical and does not by itself demonstrate operational protection or prevention."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.6","3B.7","3B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"FACT SHEET: The United We Stand Summit: Taking Action to Prevent and Address Hate-Motivated Violence and Foster Unity","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-09-15","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fact-sheet-the-united-we-stand-summit-taking-action-prevent-and-address-hate-motivated","claim":"The White House said Biden was hosting a summit to counter hate-fueled violence and rally a whole-of-society response to prevent, respond to, and recover from it.","locator":"Lines 62-67","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary White House policy fact sheet with specific actions and scope.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 policy and rhetoric on domestic violence prevention.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden-led White House initiative.","contrary_evidence":["The summit was a coordination and messaging step, not a direct remedy for local violence."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.6","3B.7","3B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Justice Department Announces New Initiatives to Address and Prevent Hate Crimes and Hate Incidents","publisher":"U.S. Department of Justice","publication_date":"2022-05-20","url":"https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-new-initiatives-address-and-prevent-hate-crimes-and-hate","claim":"DOJ announced new hate-crime guidance, state-run reporting hotlines, community-based prevention grants, and a first-ever Language Access Coordinator.","locator":"Lines 141-155","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary DOJ press release with concrete policy and grant actions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 policy response to hate violence.","ruler_attribution":"Implemented by Biden administration DOJ.","contrary_evidence":["These are support and reporting tools; they do not guarantee lower violence on their own."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.4","3B.7","3B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Justice Department Launches Nationwide Initiative to Combat Unlawful Acts of Hate","publisher":"U.S. Department of Justice","publication_date":"2022-09-15","url":"https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-launches-nationwide-initiative-combat-unlawful-acts-hate","claim":"DOJ launched United Against Hate so all 94 U.S. Attorneys' Offices would run outreach and community engagement programs to improve reporting and trust.","locator":"Lines 139-144","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary DOJ announcement with nationwide scope and specific mechanism.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 action.","ruler_attribution":"Biden administration DOJ initiative.","contrary_evidence":["The program relies on outreach and local partnerships rather than direct federal control of local violence."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.4","3B.6","3B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Associate Attorney General Vanita Gupta Delivers Remarks at the U.S. Conference of Mayors","publisher":"U.S. Department of Justice","publication_date":"2022-01-19","url":"https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/speech/associate-attorney-general-vanita-gupta-delivers-remarks-us-conference-mayors","claim":"DOJ said hate crimes and reducing gun violence were top priorities, that hate crimes had surged, and that the FBI had elevated hate crimes to its highest-level national threat priority with more than $21 million in victim and prevention grants.","locator":"Lines 146-148","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary DOJ remarks with specific policy priority and funding details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 domestic-safety policy context.","ruler_attribution":"Biden administration DOJ priorities and funding choices.","contrary_evidence":["Priority and funding do not themselves prove a large reduction in violence."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.4","3B.6","3B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Remarks on the First Anniversary of the Insurrection at the United States Capitol","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-01-06","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/remarks-the-first-anniversary-the-insurrection-the-united-states-capitol","claim":"Biden rejected political violence as a norm and framed January 6 as an assault on democracy that must not happen again.","locator":"Lines 84-86","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential remarks at a dated democratic-security event.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 rhetoric and crisis framing.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden rhetoric as President.","contrary_evidence":["This is rhetoric and norm-setting, not a direct operational protection measure."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.8","3B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Domestic Terrorism Threat One Year After January 6","publisher":"Federal Bureau of Investigation","publication_date":"2022-01-11","url":"https://www.fbi.gov/news/speeches-and-testimony/the-domestic-terrorism-threat-one-year-after-january-6-011122","claim":"FBI testimony said domestic violent extremism and hate crimes were rising, investigations had more than doubled since spring 2020, and DOJ had arrested and charged more than 725 people for the Capitol assault.","locator":"Lines 84-85","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary FBI congressional testimony with concrete threat and accountability figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 domestic-threat and accountability context.","ruler_attribution":"Biden administration federal law enforcement under his presidency.","contrary_evidence":["The threat increase predates Biden and partly reflects inherited conditions after 2020-2021."],"lenses":["3B.6","3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: United States","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/united-states","claim":"HRW said the Biden administration continued to arbitrarily expel asylum seekers under Title 42 and also applied the policy to Venezuelans, while forcing some migrants into dangerous Mexican cities under MPP.","locator":"Lines 122-124","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Well-regarded independent human-rights monitor summarizing the 2022 record, though retrospective.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on 2022 border policy and asylum treatment.","ruler_attribution":"Biden administration border enforcement policy.","contrary_evidence":["HRW notes the administration planned to terminate Title 42, but a federal judge blocked it."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.7","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Restoring Access to Asylum at the US Border","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-03-30","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/03/30/restoring-access-asylum-us-border","claim":"HRW said Biden's plans for reopening asylum at the southern border were undeveloped and contradictory and that the administration had made little progress toward a humanitarian border response.","locator":"Lines 613-614","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous independent critique of Biden's border policy direction.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 assessment of evolving border policy.","ruler_attribution":"Biden administration border policy choices.","contrary_evidence":["The article also notes Biden campaigned on humane-border goals, showing stated intent even if execution lagged."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.7","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"USA: Torture and other ill-treatment of Haitian asylum seekers is rooted in anti-Black racism","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-09-22","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/09/usa-torture-haitian-asylum-seekers-anti-black-racism/","claim":"Amnesty said Haitian asylum seekers were subjected to arbitrary detention and race-based torture-like ill-treatment, and cited Del Rio Border Patrol violence as part of the same pattern.","locator":"Lines 63-65","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent rights monitor with direct interviews and a contemporaneous report on 2022 border treatment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 evidence on border treatment and state practice.","ruler_attribution":"Biden administration border enforcement under DHS and White House authority.","contrary_evidence":["The report focuses on border enforcement and does not by itself prove Biden personally directed every abusive act."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.5","3B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Five Things to Know About the Title 42 Immigrant Expulsion Policy","publisher":"American Civil Liberties Union","publication_date":"2022-03-22","url":"https://www.aclu.org/news/immigrants-rights/five-things-to-know-about-the-title-42-immigrant-expulsion-policy","claim":"ACLU said the government had used Title 42 to kick out asylum seekers more than 1.7 million times and that the rule was in tension with Biden's stated border policy.","locator":"Lines 26-28","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Advocacy source, but the claim is specific and anchored in a policy count and date.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 criticism of the ongoing Title 42 regime.","ruler_attribution":"Biden administration continuation of a Trump-era expulsion policy.","contrary_evidence":["The policy originated under Trump, so the evidence is partly inherited rather than uniquely Biden-authored."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.7","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Biden Administration's Executive Order on Policing is a Foundation to Build Upon","publisher":"American Civil Liberties Union","publication_date":"2022-06-01","url":"https://www.aclu.org/news/criminal-law-reform/the-biden-administrations-executive-order-on-policing-is-a-foundation-to-build-upon","claim":"ACLU called Biden's police-reform executive order a starting place but said it did not go far enough to save lives.","locator":"Lines 9 and 34","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Independent contemporary critique of the federal policing reform package.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 assessment of Biden's EO 14074.","ruler_attribution":"Biden's federal policing order and its limits.","contrary_evidence":["The same source acknowledges the order was the most substantial federal police reform since Floyd's murder."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.5","3B.8","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Biden promised to reform the police. Why has so little progress been made?","publisher":"The Guardian","publication_date":"2022-12-26","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/dec/26/biden-review-police-criminal-justice-reform-promises","claim":"The Guardian argued that Biden's 2022 policing executive order was largely symbolic, had limited effect on local police, and that police killings remained near constant since 2015.","locator":"Lines 176-183","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"High-quality reporting with a clear line from the article to the policy limits and the outcome context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 retrospective on the year's police-reform record.","ruler_attribution":"Biden's federal policing agenda and its limited reach over local police.","contrary_evidence":["The article also notes the executive order was a substantial federal action and that broader reform was blocked by Congress and state-level constraints."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.5","3B.8","3B.10"]}

Search ledger: candidates found about 54, documents opened 40, duplicates collapsed 18, irrelevant or out-of-period items 8, inaccessible or technical-error items 4, accepted distinct URLs 20. Domains retained for this wave: 10.

Material gaps and next best directions:
- Stronger 2022 evidence on protest policing, crowd-control, and retaliation for dissent at the national level would improve 3B.8.
- Direct DHS, CBP, or OIG reporting on border force, detention conditions, and Title 42 implementation would sharpen 3B.2/3B.5 attribution.
- More primary evidence on whether federal accountability tools changed outcomes in 2022, not just policy design, would help 3B.4/3B.10.
- The record is strongest for federal anti-hate, anti-violence, and gun-safety action, and weakest where local policing and border enforcement were driven by inherited systems outside direct presidential control.
